# Emoji Communication Guidelines

## Context
<!-- Context to be defined: Explain when and why this rule applies. -->

These rules govern when and how we use emojis in our written communication (commit messages, pull-request descriptions, documentation, and team updates).

---

## 🔒 Critical Rules

| # | Rule |
|---|------|
| 1 | **Purpose first.** Use an emoji only when it clarifies or amplifies the message. |
| 2 | **Position:** place the emoji at the **end** of the sentence/section, never at the beginning or inline with words. |
| 3 | **Frequency:** maximum **1-2 emojis per major section** (roughly one screen-length). |
| 4 | **Tone:** remain professional; prefer clever or story-telling emojis over random celebration spam. |
| 5 | **Relevance:** choose emojis that match the theme (e.g. 🐛 for bugs, 🚀 for performance gains). |
| 6 | **Storytelling is welcome:** chaining 2-3 related emojis to create a micro-narrative is encouraged. |
| 7 | **No repetition:** avoid using the same emoji twice in the same message. |
| 8 | **No fluff:** skip confetti/cake/party emojis unless it's a genuine milestone. |

---

## ✅ Valid Examples

<example>
"I've optimized your database queries 🏃‍♂️"  
"Your bug has been squashed 🥾🐛"  
"I've cleaned up the legacy code 🧹✨"  
"Fixed the performance issue 🐌➡️🐆"
</example>

